(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["interpretation-of-medical-literature-exam-1-clinical-questions-reviews-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Clinical Questions and Reviews Quiz 2 — Interpretation of Medical Literature","category":"Interpretation of Medical Literature","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Why may the validity of a narrative review be questionable?","choices":["It is unstructured","It states its search method in detail","It weights each study by its size","It lists every excluded study and the reason"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, because with no set method for finding, choosing or appraising studies, a reader cannot judge how reliable the conclusions are. — 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Two trials enroll 250 and 750 participants. If a meta-analysis weights each in proportion to size, what share of the weight goes to the smaller trial?","choices":["50%","33%","75%","25%"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: 250 of the 1,000 total participants is 25 percent, so the larger trial contributes three times as much weight as the smaller one. — 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"How should clinicians treat a strong recommendation such as 1A?","choices":["Follow it only when a patient specifically requests it","Follow it unless a compelling rationale supports an alternative","Choose whichever approach best matches each patient's values","Apply it to a minority of patients under some circumstances"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, because strong recommendations apply to most patients in most circumstances without reservation. — 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Which findings are summarized for the studies located in a systematic review?","choices":["Journal, year and reputation of the authors","Funding source and cost of each study","Length and language of each report","Effect size, precision and validity"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and validity is judged both internally (for the sample studied) and externally (for other settings), alongside how many quality studies were found. — 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"A researcher's promotion depends on publishing a positive result. Which conflict of interest is this?","choices":["Financial conflict of interest","Institutional conflict of interest","Personal or career conflict","Regulatory conflict of interest"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: personal, intellectual or career conflicts include personal biases and professional benefits the author stands to gain. — 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"A reader asks how the studies in a systematic review were located. Which question belongs with it?","choices":["Which reviewer wrote the discussion section?","Which inclusion and exclusion criteria were used?","How many pages does the reference list run?","Were the tables printed in color?"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, because how studies were found and which criteria decided their entry are read together with how valid the original studies were. — 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"What evidence supports a 1C recommendation?","choices":["Observational studies, unsystematic experience or flawed trials","Consistent evidence from well-performed randomized trials","Randomized trials with important limitations","Overwhelming evidence from several trial designs"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and any estimate of effect from such evidence is uncertain, although benefits still appear to outweigh risks and burdens. — 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Which element may a narrative review leave out?","choices":["An expert summary of the evidence","A critique of the original research","Recommendations for clinical practice","A view across a broad range of issues"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, because narrative reviews may not critique the studies they cite, so a reader cannot tell whether the evidence behind a claim is strong. — 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Which tool is named for assessing publication and reporting bias in a review?","choices":["Nomograms","Receiver operating characteristic curves","Life tables","Funnel plots"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and the assessment is done while selecting studies, alongside the explicit criteria and the listing of excluded studies. — 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"What do good guidelines give along with their recommendations?","choices":["The authors' personal preferences","The supporting evidence and rationale","A ruling that judgment is not needed","Only the results of the largest trial"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and clinicians should still apply their own best clinical judgment when deciding whether to use a guideline in practice. — 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"How does a meta-analysis combine numerical results?","choices":["By averaging them equally whatever the size","By selecting the single largest study","By giving larger, more precise studies more weight","By counting how many studies were positive"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and the result is a more precise estimate of the effect than any single study gives; study size is the main driver of that precision. — 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A guideline gives the reasoning, the level of confidence in the evidence, and the strength of each recommendation. Which standard is met?","choices":["Evidence and strength","Description of recommendations","Systematic review","Group composition"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, because each recommendation is accompanied by its underlying reasoning, the confidence in the evidence, and its strength. — 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A guideline panel includes methodologic experts, clinicians and members of the affected population. Which standard is met?","choices":["External review","Group composition","Transparency","Updating"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, because the panel is multidisciplinary and balanced, with a variety of methodologic experts, clinicians and populations affected by the guideline. — 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A clinical question asks whether a new inhaled steroid, compared with the current one, reduces exacerbations in everyone. Which element most needs to be refined?","choices":["The comparison","The outcome","The intervention","The population"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, because 'everyone' is too broad; a more specific population, such as a defined age group or severity, should be stated for the question to be useful. — 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Under which circumstance may a meta-analysis be misleading?","choices":["The included studies all measure the same outcome","The included studies are not similar","The pooled sample is larger than any single study","The inclusion criteria were written down in advance"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and the strength of the value judgment about whether studies are similar enough to combine should be considered. — 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A meta-analysis pools only journal articles, and trials showing no benefit of a drug were never published. What is the likely effect?","choices":["The pooled benefit may be overstated","The pooled benefit will be understated","The pooled estimate will be unaffected","Heterogeneity will disappear"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, because the missing trials are the ones showing little or no effect, so the published set makes the drug look better than it is. — 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Which is an advantage of a meta-analysis?","choices":["A stronger, more precise estimate of effect size","Immunity to flaws in the included studies","No need for inclusion and exclusion criteria","Freedom from publication bias"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, because combining similar studies narrows the uncertainty around the effect compared with any one of them alone. — 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Why is a 1B recommendation graded below a 1A?","choices":["Its trials have important limitations","Its benefits do not clearly outweigh risks","It rests only on observational studies","It is a weak recommendation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, because 1B rests on randomized trials with inconsistent results, methodologic flaws or imprecision, so further research may change confidence. — 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"What may the intervention element name besides a treatment?","choices":["An outcome to be measured","The alternative to be compared","A prognostic factor or an exposure","A description of similar patients"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, because the second element covers whatever main intervention, prognostic factor or exposure is under consideration, so it suits risk and prognosis questions. — 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"For a question comparing two diagnostic tests, which outcome fits?","choices":["Number of tests ordered","Sensitivity and specificity","Patient satisfaction with the result","Length of the clinic visit"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, because a question about how well a test detects disease can name the accuracy of each test, expressed as sensitivity and specificity, as its outcome. — 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"What does the fixed effect model assume?","choices":["Effect sizes that vary for reasons beyond chance","An effect that differs in every study","No effect at all in any study","One fixed effect size, with variation due to chance"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and it is traditionally used when there is no significant heterogeneity, which means the studies appear similar enough to share one effect. — 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"What is a fundamental limitation of a meta-analysis?","choices":["It cannot combine numerical results","It always reduces the total sample size","It can address only rare complications","It is only as good as the included studies"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, because combining poor studies produces a larger, more confident poor estimate, so the quality of the pooled studies must be checked. — 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A clinical question asks whether physical therapy, compared with daily naproxen, reduces pain in adults with knee osteoarthritis. Which element is the comparison?","choices":["Physical therapy","Adults with knee osteoarthritis","Daily naproxen","Reduction in pain"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, because the comparison is the main alternative set against the intervention, here the anti-inflammatory drug that physical therapy is measured against. — 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Who should confirm that the selection of studies was consistent?","choices":["At least two reviewers","One experienced reviewer","The lead author alone","The journal editor"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, because independent agreement between reviewers checks that the criteria were applied consistently; the same is done when assessing each study's risk of bias. — 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"A review of a treatment cites only trials that favor the author's preferred therapy. Which limitation does this show?","choices":["Heterogeneity was not tested statistically","The pooled estimate was weighted by study size","The review was limited to a single narrow question","Cited articles may be chosen to prove a point"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, because with no structured method for finding studies, an author can pick the articles that support a view and leave out the rest. — 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"In a cursory review, where does the reader look to learn to whom a study applies?","choices":["The title","Patients and setting","The design","The conclusions"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, because in the cursory review the question 'to whom does it apply?' is answered from the description of the patients and setting. — 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"A clinical question asks whether nebulized hypertonic saline, compared with normal saline, shortens hospital stay in infants with bronchiolitis. Which element is the outcome?","choices":["Infants with bronchiolitis","Nebulized hypertonic saline","Length of hospital stay","Normal saline"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, because the outcome is what one hopes to accomplish, measure, improve or affect, here how long the infants remain in the hospital. — 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"A clinical question asks whether heavy alcohol use, compared with none, is linked to esophageal cancer among middle-aged smokers. Which element is the exposure?","choices":["Esophageal cancer","Middle-aged smokers","Heavy alcohol use","No alcohol use"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, because the intervention element can be an exposure or prognostic factor rather than a treatment, and here it is the factor whose effect is asked about. — 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"In an in-depth review, where does the reader look to see how big the effect was?","choices":["The introduction","The methods","Figures and tables","The discussion"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, because the size of the effect is read from the figures and tables, while methods show how strong the study was. — 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"Which consideration helps decide whether a review question can be answered?","choices":["Whether strong studies exist and agree","Whether the authors already favor an answer","Whether every study was published in one journal","Whether an expert has written on the topic before"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, because the availability of strong studies, how many there are, and whether they agree all shape whether a focused question can be answered. — 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 7"}]};
